     1	# ErgoWorks Consulting Campaign Plan
     2	
     3	## 1. Executive summary
     4	
     5	ErgoWorks Consulting already has a mature Google Search program. This plan is an optimisation and expansion programme—not a greenfield launch.
     6	
     7	The recommended strategy is to:
     8	
     9	1. **Protect and improve existing Search demand capture** by cleaning conversion goals, auditing query quality, strengthening Brand coverage and routing paid traffic to relevant landing-page variants.
    10	2. **Open Meta as the missing demand-generation lever** using workplace- and role-level creative, beginning with one consolidated prospecting ad set.
    11	3. **Launch a dedicated conversion landing page** centred on the free 15-minute Workplace Ergonomics Risk Snapshot—**preliminary guidance only—not a compliance assessment, compliance certification, medical assessment or diagnosis**.
    12	4. **Connect media to qualified pipeline outcomes** through CRM stages, offline conversion imports, server-side tagging and Meta CAPI.
    13	5. **Keep PMax and paid remarketing parked unless their funding, measurement, privacy and isolation gates can be met.**
    14	
    15	The preferred minimum launch envelope is **A$4,846 per 30-day planning period**: preserve the verified A$4,246 last-30-day Search baseline and add A$600 for one Meta prospecting ad set. This remains subject to commercial approval, Snapshot delivery capacity, landing-page readiness and the Australian privacy gate.
    16	
    17	No target CPA, kill decision or scale commitment is defensible until conversion pollution is removed and average contract value, delivery margin and qualified-lead outcomes are known.
    18	
    19	## 2. The big idea
    20	
    21	### See the risk. Act on the priorities. Prove implementation.
    22	
    23	ErgoWorks should sell a practical implementation path rather than an isolated assessment:
    24	
    25	| Strategic layer | Role |
    26	|---|---|
    27	| Optimise existing Search | Capture established commercial demand more accurately and efficiently |
    28	| Add Meta demand generation | Reach WHS/HSE, HR/People & Culture, Facilities and Operations buyers before they search |
    29	| Introduce the Snapshot | Give cold prospects a defined, low-commitment first step |
    30	| Use a dedicated landing page | Replace generic WordPress destinations with message-matched conversion journeys |
    31	| Fix measurement | Optimise toward qualified Consulting opportunities rather than newsletters and soft actions |
    32	| Evaluate PMax and remarketing | Activate only when properly funded, isolated and measurable |
    33	
    34	Search remains the proven demand-capture engine. Meta adds the top-of-funnel reach that the current programme lacks. The landing page and Snapshot connect those channels to a coherent commercial journey, while the measurement rebuild ensures future allocation is based on qualified pipeline rather than raw platform conversions.
    35	
    36	## 3. Current-state snapshot
    37	
    38	### Verified account baseline
    39	
    40	| Item | Verified position |
    41	|---|---|
    42	| Google Ads account | **9258098368** |
    43	| GA4 property | **316175981** |
    44	| Focus campaign | **700072710 — Ergoworks Consulting Search** |
    45	| Bidding | **Maximize Conversions**, no target CPA, standard strategy with no portfolio |
    46	| Existing foundations | Auto-tagging on; GA4-linked conversions |
    47	| Search investment | Approximately **A$40,000 trailing 12 months** |
    48	| Observed monthly range | Approximately **A$3.0k–A$4.7k per month** |
    49	| Last 30 days | **A$4,246 spend; 4,540 impressions; 407 clicks; 26 reported conversions** |
    50	| Estimated real leads | Approximately **20 in the last 30 days** |
    51	| Indicative real-lead cost | Approximately **A$212 per lead** |
    52	| Paid social | **Zero paid-social activity in GA4 over the last 90 days** |
    53	| PMax | Campaign 16143562968 paused |
    54	| Display remarketing | Campaign 843760968 paused |
    55	
    56	The 26 reported last-30-day conversions include:
    57	
    58	- 10 Calls from ads
    59	- 10 Make an Enquiry conversions
    60	- 4 Newsletter Sign Ups counted as Primary
    61	- 1 Email Click
    62	- 1 Call Forwarding
    63	
    64	This means reported conversion volume overstates genuine leads and currently gives Maximize Conversions an impure optimisation signal. There is no qualified-lead or closed-won import.
    65	
    66	### Search concentration
    67	
    68	Manual Handling Training received **A$2,069—49% of the A$4,246 last-30-day spend**. Manual handling training remains the current spend anchor and largest verified **TRAINING** term at **2,700 Australian searches per month**; research also identifies an approximately **3,400-searches-per-month mixed-intent certificate/course pool** with buyer type **UNCLASSIFIED** and an approximately **780-searches-per-month care-sector opportunity**. These are directional demand estimates, not forecasts or verified service fit, and both expansion pockets remain gated as set out in Appendices A–D.
    69	
    70	Other notable last-30-day results include:
    71	
    72	| Ad group | Spend | Reported conversions | Interpretation |
    73	|---|---:|---:|---|
    74	| Manual Handling Training | A$2,069 | 10.5 | Dominant demand, but query and lead quality are unverified |
    75	| “DSA — Ergoworks Consulting” | A$795 | 4.5 | Not DSA: nine static EXACT terms form a competitor/generic group; total quality is unverified, while only the sampled government/competitor-query subset was near-zero converting |
    76	| Ergonomic Office Consulting | A$719 | 7 | Reported CPA approximately A$103; validate conversion quality before expansion |
    77	| Ergonomics Assessments | A$531 | 3 | Diagnose after conversion cleanup |
    78	| Workplace Ergonomics | A$133 | 1 | Insufficient evidence for a directional decision |
    79	| Brand | A$0 | 0 | All three dedicated Brand terms are paused; the same three terms are duplicated across five service groups, so repair requires in-place re-enablement and de-duplication before any breakout decision |
    80	
    81	Paid traffic currently lands on generic WordPress pages, led by the Manual Handling Training page with 234 clicks in the last 30 days. There is no dedicated paid-media conversion page.
    82	
    83	### Scope limitation
    84	
    85	The available evidence includes last-30-day search terms, the full keyword inventory with match types, statuses and headline Quality Scores, 493 negatives and a partial geography signal. Remaining gaps are longer search-term and keyword-performance history, the negative-list audit, RSAs/assets, complete campaign settings, Quality Score components and geo/device performance reports. Consequently, the package supports a diagnostic plan—not unconditional pause, restructure or scale instructions.
    86	
    87	The shared Ads account also contains Physiotherapy activity. Consulting goals, tags, audiences and campaign controls must be isolated without applying account-wide negatives or exclusions that could damage Physiotherapy.
    88	
    89	## 4. Offer and positioning
    90	
    91	### Core position
    92	
    93	ErgoWorks should be positioned as the workplace-ergonomics implementation partner that helps organisations:
    94	
    95	- establish visibility over workplace-ergonomics priorities;
    96	- translate findings into practical actions and responsibilities;
    97	- create repeatable implementation and reporting evidence.
    98	
    99	The promise is controllable outputs—not guaranteed injury prevention, assured WHS compliance, claims reductions or medical outcomes.
   100	
   101	### Canonical offer registry — client approval required before launch
   102	
   103	| Stage | Offer | Eligibility / best fit | Exact deliverable and pricing status | Required caveat |
   104	|---|---|---|---|---|
   105	| Entry | **Free 15-minute Workplace Ergonomics Risk Snapshot** | B2B organisational buyer with one defined workplace-ergonomics question; capacity and service geography must be approved | 15-minute consultant conversation and written three-point summary; free, subject to approved fulfilment capacity | Preliminary guidance only—not a compliance assessment, compliance certification, medical assessment or diagnosis |
   106	| Paid entry | **ErgoStart** | Defined site, cohort or issue | Fixed-scope assessment sprint and prioritised actions; pricing unconfirmed and must not be advertised until client-approved | Scope, inclusions, delivery timing and price are confirmed only in an approved proposal |
   107	| Rollout | **ErgoScale** | Corporate, multi-site or hybrid programme | Repeatable workflow, implementation plan and reporting; pricing unconfirmed | Coverage, timing and outputs depend on the approved programme scope |
   108	| Capability | **ErgoCoach** | Manual-task or train-the-trainer need | Task-led review, tailored training and supervisor toolkit; pricing unconfirmed | Format, participants and outputs depend on the approved scope |
   109	| Ongoing | **ErgoControl** | Organisation requiring ongoing programme visibility | ErgoAssess subscription or managed retainer, review cadence and reporting; pricing unconfirmed | Platform, service level, cadence and outputs depend on the approved scope |
   110	
   111	The Snapshot gives cold prospects a concrete next step without asking them to commit immediately to a full programme. The offer names, eligibility, fulfilment promise and ladder remain **client sign-off required**.
   112	
   113	Detailed positioning and creative direction are in [Appendix D](./appendices/D-creative-briefs.md).
   114	
   115	## 5. Channel and budget at a glance
   116	
   117	All budgets use a **30-day planning period**. They are anchored to both the approximately **A$40,000 trailing-12-month Search investment** and the observed **A$3.0k–A$4.7k monthly range**, with the verified **A$4,246 last-30-day spend** as the exact planning baseline.
   118	
   119	| Scenario | Search | Meta | PMax | Remarketing | Total | Recommendation |
   120	|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---|
   121	| **1. Flat-budget reallocation** | A$3,646 | A$600 | A$0 | A$0 | **A$4,246** | Use only if total media must remain flat |
   122	| **2. Minimum Meta on top** | A$4,246 | A$600 | A$0 | A$0 | **A$4,846** | **Preferred minimum launch scenario** |
   123	| **3. Meta plus funded PMax** | A$4,246 | A$600 | A$1,500 | A$0 | **A$6,346** | Consider only after all PMax gates pass |
   124	
   125	### Platform floors
   126	
   127	| Channel | Planning floor | Implication |
   128	|---|---:|---|
   129	| Meta | **A$20/day or A$600 per ad set per 30 days** | Keep the initial budget in one consolidated prospecting ad set |
   130	| PMax | **A$50/day or A$1,500 per 30 days** | A nominal 5–10% allocation is not viable at the current run-rate |
   131	| Paid remarketing | Separate viable budget plus sufficient audience size | Build audiences first; park paid delivery initially |
   132	
   133	The provisional Meta Snapshot-request CPA planning range of **A$53.08–A$84.92** is assumption-based, not observed performance or a commercial target. It assumes the lower-friction Snapshot request costs 25%–40% of the current approximately A$212 raw Search lead cost. It must be replaced with observed data.
   134	
   135	PMax should be **funded to its floor or parked**. Across the observed A$3.0k–A$4.7k monthly range, its floor would consume approximately 31.9%–50.0% of the budget. Paid remarketing should not share PMax’s A$1,500 testing allocation.
   136	
   137	Detailed channel structure, keywords, floors and conditional decision tables are in [Appendix B](./appendices/B-keyword-adgroup-sheets.md). The existing-account diagnostic is in [Appendix E](./appendices/E-existing-account-audit.md).
   138	
   139	## 6. Landing-page concept
   140	
   141	Build one dedicated, navigation-reduced landing-page system on an approved ErgoWorks Consulting destination.
   142	
   143	### Page principles
   144	
   145	- **Launch default:** one dominant CTA on every baseline variant: request the free 15-minute Workplace Ergonomics Risk Snapshot—preliminary guidance only, **not a compliance assessment, compliance certification, medical assessment or diagnosis**. Service-plan-primary variants are post-baseline tests only.
   146	- Message-matched variants led by:
   147	
   148	  1. Manual Handling Training
   149	  2. Workstation and Ergonomic Assessment
   150	  3. Hybrid and Home-office programmes
   151	
   152	- Stripped header with ErgoWorks identity, an optional phone contact and no store or Physiotherapy navigation.
   153	- Two-step form:
   154	
   155	  1. business contact details;
   156	  2. non-sensitive qualification fields such as team-size band, service need, location and timeframe.
   157	
   158	- No injury history, symptoms, diagnoses, treatment, claim details or other health information.
   159	- Separate required enquiry consent from optional, unchecked marketing consent.
   160	- Clear explanation of what the Snapshot includes, what happens next and what it does not represent.
   161	- Mobile-first performance, source/click-ID capture and a clear thank-you journey.
   162	- Proof only after substantiation and written client approval.
   163	
   164	The complete page architecture and copy are in [Appendix A](./appendices/A-landing-page-copy.md).
   165	
   166	## 7. Measurement approach
   167	
   168	Measurement is an upgrade to existing GA4-linked tracking—not a rebuild from zero.
   169	
   170	### Conversion hierarchy
   171	
   172	| Stage | Treatment |
   173	|---|---|
   174	| Valid Consulting enquiry | Primary initially |
   175	| Qualified new-business Consulting call | Primary only when qualification rules can be enforced or reconciled |
   176	| Valid B2B Snapshot request | Primary initially; preliminary guidance only—not a compliance assessment, compliance certification, medical assessment or diagnosis |
   177	| CRM-qualified lead | Primary offline import once reliable |
   178	| Proposal and closed-won | Downstream/value signals as volume and data quality permit |
   179	| Newsletter, email click, click-to-call, call forwarding and local actions | Secondary |
   180	| Physiotherapy actions | Excluded from Consulting campaign goals |
   181	
   182	### Measurement stack
   183	
   184	- Preserve Google auto-tagging and the existing GA4 link.
   185	- Use campaign-specific Consulting conversion goals.
   186	- Capture GCLID, GBRAID, WBRAID, FBCLID and UTMs in the CRM.
   187	- Send qualified-lead, proposal and closed-won outcomes through Google Data Manager and Meta CAPI.
   188	- Implement server-side GTM on a first-party domain such as `tags.ergoworksconsulting.com.au`.
   189	- Use a Conversion Linker and Meta Pixel plus CAPI, preferably through CAPI Gateway.
   190	- Use matching browser/server `event_id` values for deduplication.
   191	- Use Data-Driven Attribution where eligible; do not use rule-based attribution.
   192	- Implement **Consent Mode v2, default denied, with no client- or server-side analytics/advertising transmission before consent**, through an approved CMP.
   193	- Enforce no pre-consent advertising or analytics transmissions unless Australian privacy/legal review explicitly approves a different configuration.
   194	- Keep Consulting separate from Physiotherapy, ErgoEquip and Biosymm through dedicated or tightly scoped GA4 stream, Meta Dataset/Pixel, GTM triggers, audiences and CRM mappings.
   195	
   196	### First-90-day measurement commitments
   197	
   198	- 100% of approved Primary actions documented and tested.
   199	- Newsletter and soft actions removed from Consulting bidding goals.
   200	- Physiotherapy actions excluded from campaign 700072710 goals.
   201	- CRM lead IDs and event IDs captured on valid lead records.
   202	- Offline-event latency below 24 hours where operationally possible; 24–72 hours is a warning range.
   203	- Meta lead-event EMQ of at least 8.0.
   204	- Browser/server deduplication of at least 90%.
   205	- Server/client hit ratio between 80% and 120%.
   206	- At least six applicable Meta match parameters on eligible events.
   207	- Weekly platform-to-CRM reconciliation.
   208	- Zero sensitive-data events or parameters detected in testing.
   209	
   210	The primary commercial KPI is **cost per qualified lead (CPQL)**. Raw CPL remains an entry-stage diagnostic. CAC and target CPQL cannot be set until contract value, contribution margin and stage-conversion rates are verified.
   211	
   212	The full event taxonomy, consent requirements, field allowlist/deny list and QA plan are in [Appendix C](./appendices/C-tracking-spec.md).
   213	
   214	### Methodology conflict
   215	
   216	The generic ads-budget rubric recommends Maximize Clicks below 30 conversions per month. The live campaign, however, is mature, already uses Maximize Conversions and generates approximately 20 real leads per month while its current conversion signal is polluted.
   217	
   218	Automatically switching to Maximize Clicks during measurement repair could optimise for traffic volume before query quality is understood. The account-specific recommendation is therefore to retain Maximize Conversions while cleaning and restabilising the baseline, then reassess:
   219	
   220	| Clean post-remediation condition | Bidding decision |
     1	# Appendix D — Positioning, Offer Ladder, Messaging Matrix & Creative Briefs
     2	
     3	## Strategic role
     4	
     5	ErgoWorks is not starting from zero. Google Ads account **9258098368** already contains a mature Consulting Search campaign (**700072710**) spending approximately **A$40,000 trailing 12 months**, with an observed range of **A$3.0k–A$4.7k per month** and approximately **20 real leads per month**. The campaign uses **Maximize Conversions with no target CPA**.
     6	
     7	Search captures existing demand; Meta must create demand that does not yet exist. In the last 90 days, GA4 property **316175981** recorded **zero paid-social activity** and only three Organic Social sessions. Paid traffic also lacks a dedicated conversion landing page and currently reaches generic WordPress service pages.
     8	
     9	The creative strategy therefore has three jobs:
    10	
    11	1. Improve message match within the existing Search program.
    12	2. Introduce the Snapshot and implementation pathway through Meta demand generation.
    13	3. Move buyers from a visible workplace issue to prioritised action and evidence of implementation.
    14	
    15	No campaign, creative, landing page, pixel, form, or live account should be changed until client approval, measurement repair, and the pre-launch privacy gate are complete.
    16	
    17	---
    18	
    19	## Positioning
    20	
    21	### Core position
    22	
    23	> **See the risk. Act on the priorities. Prove implementation.**
    24	
    25	ErgoWorks should be positioned as the workplace-ergonomics implementation partner for organisations that need to turn scattered concerns into a clear, practical and trackable programme.
    26	
    27	The commercial story is not “buy an assessment.” It is:
    28	
    29	| Stage | Buyer question | ErgoWorks answer | Controllable output |
    30	|---|---|---|---|
    31	| See the risk | What needs attention? | Establish a consistent view of workstation, hybrid-work or manual-task priorities | Defined scope, observations and prioritised findings |
    32	| Act on priorities | What should happen next? | Translate findings into practical controls, training, equipment guidance and manager actions | Documented action plan, responsibilities and recommended next steps |
    33	| Prove implementation | How do we show progress? | Establish repeatable workflows, action-status records and programme reporting | Coverage, action status and implementation evidence |
    34	
    35	This positioning does **not** promise injury prevention, WHS compliance, medical outcomes, reduced claims, or a quantified financial result.
    36	
    37	### Positioning statement
    38	
    39	> For WHS/HSE, HR/People & Culture and Facilities teams that need a practical way to manage workplace-ergonomics priorities, ErgoWorks provides a defined path from risk visibility to prioritised action and implementation evidence—across workstations, hybrid workplaces and manual tasks.
    40	
    41	### Buyer priorities
    42	
    43	| Buyer | Primary concern | Positioning emphasis | Useful proof after verification |
    44	|---|---|---|---|
    45	| WHS/HSE | Visibility, prioritisation and evidence of action | Consistent assessment approach, practical controls, documented implementation | Anonymised action plan, programme coverage, professional credentials |
    46	| HR / People & Culture | Employee support, hybrid consistency and manageable rollout | Clear employee journey, scalable communications and defined follow-up | Completion rates, rollout example, employee guidance sample |
    47	| Facilities / Workplace Experience | Workspace, equipment and fitout decisions | Prioritised workspace recommendations and documented design inputs | Sample workspace brief, multi-site example, before/after workflow |
    48	| Operations / Risk | Manual-task consistency and supervisor capability | Task-led review, tailored training and repeatable internal capability | Training outline, supervisor toolkit, implementation records |
    49	
    50	### Trigger-to-outcome framing
    51	
    52	| Trigger | Safe problem framing | Outcome to sell |
    53	|---|---|---|
    54	| Workers-compensation cost pressure | Leaders need clearer visibility into ergonomic priorities and actions | Prioritised controls and implementation reporting |
    55	| Return-to-office or hybrid change | Work settings and employee support may be inconsistent across locations | A repeatable office-and-home programme |
    56	| Office fitout or relocation | Workspace decisions need practical ergonomic input before implementation | A documented workspace and equipment brief |
    57	| WHS audit or internal review | Teams need organised evidence of what was reviewed and acted upon | Defined findings, owners and action-status records |
    58	| Recurring discomfort complaints | Repeated workplace concerns can be difficult to triage consistently | A structured pathway for reviewing workplace factors and identifying next steps |
    59	
    60	The phrase “discomfort complaints” may describe an organisational trigger. Meta ads must not infer that the person viewing the ad, or their employees, has a health condition.
    61	
    62	### Differentiation
    63	
    64	ErgoWorks should differentiate from assessment-only providers, occupational-rehabilitation bundles and software-only alternatives through the implementation path:
    65	
    66	- Consulting expertise for defining and prioritising the issue.
    67	- Productised delivery rather than open-ended assessment activity.
    68	- Training and internal capability for practical follow-through.
    69	- ErgoAssess-enabled workflows for ongoing visibility.
    70	- Australia-wide applicability, subject to confirmation of delivery coverage before publishing.
    71	
    72	Any use of awards, customer logos, the Danone name, “ASX 100,” a 5.0/63-review claim, named-company results, or similar proof is **client sign-off required**.
    73	
    74	---
    75	
